## Module 4: Precision Optimization with `useMemo` & `useCallback`

### 4.1 Caching Computationally Expensive Operations with `useMemo`
`useMemo` caches the **result** of a calculation across render passes.

$$\text{Time Complexity Reduction: } \mathcal{O}(n \log n) \xrightarrow{\text{Re-renders without prop changes}} \mathcal{O}(1)$$

```javascript
const PeopleList = ({ unsortedPeople }) => {
  const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState("");

  // ❌ WITHOUT useMemo: Sorts 1,000,000 items on EVERY single keystroke in setSearchTerm!
  // Time Complexity: O(n log n) per keypress.
  // const sortedPeople = [...unsortedPeople].sort((a, b) => b.age - a.age);

  //  WITH useMemo: Only sorts when `unsortedPeople` reference changes!
  const sortedPeople = useMemo(() => {
    return [...unsortedPeople].sort((a, b) => b.age - a.age);
  }, [unsortedPeople]);

  return (
    <div>
      <input value={searchTerm} onChange={e => setSearchTerm(e.target.value)} />
      <ul>
        {sortedPeople.map(p => <li key={p.id}>{p.name} - {p.age}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};
```

---

### 4.2 `useMemo` Considered Harmful: Over-Optimization Anti-Patterns

Wrapping scalar (primitive) calculations in `useMemo` is a common performance anti-pattern.

```javascript
// ❌ ANTI-PATTERN: Wrapping simple scalar arithmetic in useMemo
const MyComponent = ({ count }) => {
  // Overhead of allocating the arrow function + invoking useMemo + dependency array comparison
  // exceeds the cost of simple integer multiplication!
  const doubled = useMemo(() => count * 2, [count]);

  return <div>{doubled}</div>;
};

//  CORRECT: Compute scalar values directly inline
const MyComponent = ({ count }) => {
  const doubled = count * 2; // Negligible CPU cost (sub-nanosecond execution)
  return <div>{doubled}</div>;
};
```

---

### 4.3 Function Reference Stability with `useCallback`
`useCallback` is syntactic sugar for `useMemo` specialized for function references:

$$\text{useCallback}(\text{fn}, \text{deps}) \equiv \text{useMemo}(() \Rightarrow \text{fn}, \text{deps})$$

#### The Native Host Element Misconception:
Passing inline functions to native host elements (like `<button>`, `<div>`, `<input>`) does **not** cause performance issues, and wrapping them in `useCallback` is an anti-pattern.

```javascript
// ❌ USELESS USECALLBACK: Provides zero performance benefit
function MyComponent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Anti-pattern: useCallback overhead for a native DOM element
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    setCount(c => c + 1);
  }, []);

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Increment</button>;
}
```

#### Why `useCallback` on Native Elements is Useless:
1. Native elements (host components) do not run custom React render functions.
2. React handles native events via **Event Delegation** at the root container.
3. Updating an `onClick` prop on a native `<button>` DOM element simply updates the internal event listener reference in React's event registry—it does **not** trigger a re-render cascade.

#### Valid Use Cases for `useCallback`:
1. Passing callbacks to custom child components optimized with `React.memo`.
2. Passing functions as dependency items to other hooks (`useEffect`, `useMemo`).

```javascript
//  VALID USE CASE: Preventing MemoizedChild from re-rendering
const MemoizedChild = React.memo(({ onItemSelect }) => {
  return <button onClick={onItemSelect}>Select</button>;
});

function Parent({ itemId }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Prevents onItemSelect reference from changing when `count` updates
  const handleSelect = useCallback(() => {
    console.log("Selected item:", itemId);
  }, [itemId]);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Inc: {count}</button>
      <MemoizedChild onItemSelect={handleSelect} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

---

## Module 5: The Future of Memoization: React Forget (React Compiler)

Manual memoization using `React.memo`, `useMemo`, and `useCallback` places a significant cognitive load on developers and leads to brittle code. To address this, the React core team developed **React Forget** (the **React Compiler**).

```
Manual Approach (Legacy):
[ Developer ] ──> Manually calculates deps ──> Wraps with useMemo / useCallback / React.memo

React Compiler Approach (Modern):
[ Developer ] ──> Writes clean, idiomatic JS/JSX
                       │
                       ▼
            ( React Compiler Pass )
                       │
                       ▼
[ Auto-Memoized JS ] ──> Automatically injects semantic memoization at build time
```

### 5.1 How React Forget Works
React Forget is an auto-memoizing build-time compiler toolchain (Babel/SWC plugin). It analyzes code using static analysis and intermediate representation (IR) transformations.

Instead of relying on **reference identity** (`===`), the compiler restructures the output to update variables based on **semantic value changes**:

```javascript
// --- Source Code Written by Developer ---
function Component({ a, b }) {
  const c = doExpensiveWork(a);
  return <Child c={c} b={b} />;
}

// --- Output Generated by React Compiler (Simplified Concept) ---
function Component(props) {
  const $ = useMemoCache(4); // Fixed-size cache array allocated per Fiber
  let c;
  if ($[0] !== props.a) {
    c = doExpensiveWork(props.a);
    $[0] = props.a;
    $[1] = c;
  } else {
    c = $[1];
  }

  let t0;
  if ($[2] !== c || $[3] !== props.b) {
    t0 = <Child c={c} b={props.b} />;
    $[2] = c;
    $[3] = props.b;
    $[4] = t0;
  } else {
    t0 = $[4];
  }
  return t0;
}
```

> [!NOTE]
> The React Compiler shifts memoization from a **manual design choice** to an **automated runtime property**, eliminating `useMemo`, `useCallback`, and `React.memo` from application code bases while ensuring maximum performance by default.

