## Module 6: Code Splitting & Lazy Loading (`React.lazy` & `Suspense`)

As applications scale, JavaScript bundle sizes grow linearly. Shipping megabytes of JavaScript causes severe network delays and long parse/compilation times on the main thread.

```
Monolithic Bundle (Slow Initial Load):
[ App Bundle (15MB) ] ──> Download ──> Parse ──> Compile ──> Execute ──> Render UI (Latency: 5s)

Code-Split Bundles (Optimized):
[ Core Shell (200KB) ] ──> Immediate Render (Latency: 200ms)
       │
       └──> Dynamic import() ──> [ Feature Module (1.5MB) ] (Loaded On-Demand)
```

### 6.1 Dynamic Imports and ES Modules
Standard ES module imports (`import Module from './Module'`) are static and evaluated at compile time. 

Dynamic imports (`import('./Module')`) are evaluated at runtime and return a native JavaScript **Promise** that resolves to the module namespace object.

```javascript
// Native Dynamic Import
button.addEventListener('click', () => {
  import('./analyticsModule.js')
    .then((analytics) => {
      analytics.trackClick();
    })
    .catch((err) => console.error("Failed to load module", err));
});
```

---

### 6.2 `React.lazy` and Suspense Architecture
`React.lazy` wraps a dynamic `import()` promise and converts it into a React component that can be rendered inside the Virtual DOM tree.

When `React.lazy` renders for the first time, its internal Promise is **pending**. It **throws the pending promise** up the component tree. The nearest `<Suspense>` boundary catches this thrown promise, pauses rendering of that subtree, and renders its `fallback` UI until the promise resolves.

```javascript
import React, { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';

// 1. Lazy-load the heavy component chunk (separate HTTP request)
const HeavyDataGrid = lazy(() => import('./HeavyDataGrid'));

function AnalyticsDashboard() {
  const [showGrid, setShowGrid] = useState(false);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setShowGrid(true)}>Load Analytics Grid</button>

      {showGrid && (
        // 2. Suspense catches the pending promise thrown by HeavyDataGrid
        <Suspense fallback={<GridSkeletonLoader />}>
          <HeavyDataGrid />
        </Suspense>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### 6.3 Granular vs. Macro Suspense Boundaries

```
 Macro Suspense Boundary (Bad UX):
 <Suspense fallback={<FullPageSpinner />}>
   <Header />
   <Sidebar />  <-- Lazy loaded (Blocks entire app from displaying!)
   <MainContent />
 </Suspense>

 Micro / Granular Suspense Boundary (Good UX):
 <Header />
 <MainContent />
 <Suspense fallback={<SidebarSkeleton />}>
   <Sidebar />  <-- Lazy loaded (Only local sidebar displays skeleton)
 </Suspense>
```

> [!TIP]
> Place `<Suspense>` boundaries as close to the lazy-loaded components as possible. Wrapping high-level parent components in a single Suspense boundary causes the entire UI to be replaced by a loading fallback, creating a disruptive user experience.

