## Module 8: Masterclass: Advanced React Design Patterns

Design patterns provide structured, repeatable solutions to common architectural problems. Below is an analysis of the seven primary React design patterns.

```
                             ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
                             │       React Design Patterns          │
                             └──────────────────┬───────────────────┘
                                                │
 ┌──────────────────────┬───────────────────────┼───────────────────────┬──────────────────────┐
 ▼                      ▼                       ▼                       ▼                      ▼
Container/              Higher-Order            Render Props /          Control Props          Compound Components /
Presentational          Components (HOCs)       Prop Getters                                   State Reducers
```

---

### Pattern 1: Presentational & Container Components
Decouples data-fetching/state management logic (Container) from rendering markup and layout (Presentational).

```javascript
// Presentational Component: Pure UI, zero state logic
const CounterView = ({ count, onIncrement, onDecrement }) => (
  <section className="counter-card">
    <h1>Count: {count}</h1>
    <button onClick={onIncrement}>+</button>
    <button onClick={onDecrement}>-</button>
  </section>
);

// Container Component: Manages state and passes handlers to View
const CounterContainer = () => {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    <CounterView
      count={count}
      onIncrement={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}
      onDecrement={() => setCount(c => c - 1)}
    />
  );
};
```

---

### Pattern 2: Higher-Order Components (HOCs)
A function that receives a component as an argument and returns an enhanced component. Used for cross-cutting concerns (e.g., authentication, logging, error handling).

```javascript
// HOC Factory for Handling Asynchronous Data Loading
const withAsyncLoading = (WrappedComponent) => {
  return ({ isLoading, error, ...passThroughProps }) => {
    if (isLoading) return <div className="spinner">Loading...</div>;
    if (error) return <div className="error-alert">{error.message}</div>;
    return <WrappedComponent {...passThroughProps} />;
  };
};

// Usage
const UserProfileWithAsync = withAsyncLoading(UserProfile);
```

#### Functional HOC Composition via `reduceRight`:
When nesting multiple HOCs, function composition flattens execution:

```javascript
const compose = (...hocs) => (BaseComponent) =>
  hocs.reduceRight((acc, hoc) => hoc(acc), BaseComponent);

// Clean composition pipeline
const EnhancedDashboard = compose(
  withAuthentication,
  withLogging,
  withAsyncLoading
)(DashboardView);
```

---

### Pattern 3: Render Props & Prop Getters

#### Render Props
Inverts rendering control to the parent component by accepting a function that returns JSX.

```javascript
const MouseTracker = ({ render }) => {
  const [position, setPosition] = useState({ x: 0, y: 0 });

  const handleMouseMove = (e) => setPosition({ x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY });

  return (
    <div style={{ height: '100vh' }} onMouseMove={handleMouseMove}>
      {render(position)}
    </div>
  );
};

// Usage: Parent controls the rendered UI
<MouseTracker render={({ x, y }) => <h1>Cursor at: {x}, {y}</h1>} />
```

#### Prop Getters
Extends prop collections by merging user-defined custom callbacks with default component functionality.

```javascript
const useDroppable = () => {
  // Utility to compose multiple functions into one execution chain
  const composeFunctions = (...fns) => (...args) =>
    fns.forEach(fn => fn && fn(...args));

  const getDroppableProps = ({ onDragOver, ...customProps } = {}) => {
    const defaultOnDragOver = (e) => {
      e.preventDefault(); // Default required drag behavior
    };

    return {
      onDragOver: composeFunctions(defaultOnDragOver, onDragOver),
      ...customProps,
    };
  };

  return { getDroppableProps };
};
```

---

### Pattern 4: Control Props Pattern
Allows a component to operate in two modes: **Uncontrolled** (manages its own internal state) or **Controlled** (state is driven externally via props).

```javascript
function Toggle({ isControlledOn, onToggle }) {
  const [internalOn, setInternalOn] = useState(false);

  // Determine controlled vs uncontrolled state
  const isControlled = isControlledOn !== undefined;
  const isOn = isControlled ? isControlledOn : internalOn;

  const handleToggle = () => {
    if (!isControlled) {
      setInternalOn(!internalOn);
    }
    if (onToggle) {
      onToggle(!isOn);
    }
  };

  return (
    <button onClick={handleToggle}>
      {isOn ? "ON" : "OFF"}
    </button>
  );
}
```

---

### Pattern 5: Compound Components Pattern
Creates a group of interconnected components that share implicit state via React Context, enabling a clean, flexible JSX structure.

```javascript
const AccordionContext = createContext();

// Parent Compound Component
export function Accordion({ children }) {
  const [openIndex, setOpenIndex] = useState(0);

  return (
    <AccordionContext.Provider value={{ openIndex, setOpenIndex }}>
      <div className="accordion-group">{children}</div>
    </AccordionContext.Provider>
  );
}

// Child Compound Component
export function AccordionItem({ index, title, children }) {
  const { openIndex, setOpenIndex } = useContext(AccordionContext);
  const isOpen = openIndex === index;

  return (
    <div className="accordion-item">
      <button onClick={() => setOpenIndex(isOpen ? null : index)}>
        {title}
      </button>
      {isOpen && <div className="accordion-content">{children}</div>}
    </div>
  );
}

// Usage: Clean, declarative composition
<Accordion>
  <AccordionItem index={0} title="Section 1">Content 1</AccordionItem>
  <hr /> {/* Native element interspersed freely! */}
  <AccordionItem index={1} title="Section 2">Content 2</AccordionItem>
</Accordion>
```

