## Concept Check & Interactive Quiz

Test your understanding of the concepts covered in this chapter.

<details>
<summary><b>Question 1:</b> Why does <code>React.memo</code> fail to prevent a child component from re-rendering when an inline arrow function or object literal is passed to it as a prop?</summary>

**Answer:** 
`React.memo` performs a shallow comparison (`shallowEqual`) of incoming props against previous props. In JavaScript, functions and object literals are non-primitive (reference) types. Every time a parent component renders, inline objects and functions are re-allocated at new memory addresses in the heap. When `shallowEqual` compares two object or function references located at different heap addresses, it evaluates to `false`, causing `React.memo` to skip bailout and trigger a re-render.
</details>

<details>
<summary><b>Question 2:</b> Under what internal condition will a component wrapped in <code>React.memo</code> re-render even if its props have not changed at all?</summary>

**Answer:** 
A memoized component will re-render despite unchanged props if:
1. It subscribes to a **React Context** via `useContext` or `<Context.Consumer>`, and that context value changes. (The reconciler's `checkScheduledUpdateOrContext` detects the context update and overrides the prop bailout).
2. The component manages internal local state (`useState` or `useReducer`), and a state update is dispatched directly inside it.
</details>

<details>
<summary><b>Question 3:</b> What is the fundamental operational difference between <code>useMemo</code> and <code>useCallback</code>?</summary>

**Answer:** 
`useMemo` caches the **evaluated result value** of a calculation function across renders. `useCallback` caches the **function reference itself** across renders. Syntactically, `useCallback(fn, deps)` is identical to `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`.
</details>

<details>
<summary><b>Question 4:</b> Explain the mechanism of how <code>React.lazy</code> interacts with <code>Suspense</code> boundaries during the Render Phase.</summary>

**Answer:** 
When a `React.lazy` component renders while its dynamic `import()` is still pending, it throws a pending **Promise** object. React's rendering engine catches this thrown promise at the nearest parent `<Suspense>` boundary. The Suspense boundary catches the promise, pauses the rendering pass for that component subtree, and renders its `fallback` UI. When the dynamic import promise resolves, React restarts the render pass for the lazy component subtree, replacing the fallback with the loaded component.
</details>

<details>
<summary><b>Question 5:</b> How does the State Reducer pattern provide greater flexibility than standard state management?</summary>

**Answer:** 
The State Reducer pattern allows a component to invert control of its internal state transitions to the parent consumer. By passing a `stateReducer` callback prop, the parent can intercept action dispatches, inspect proposed state changes (`action.changes`), and conditionally modify or reject the state update before it is committed to internal state.
</details>

